WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.910
In this video, you chat with the model that vLLM serves on the GPU cluster, then follow the GPU and vLLM metrics of that work in SUSE Observability.

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:16.330
Open WebUI from the SUSE AI stack greets you with its release notes. Close them.

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:23.510
Open WebUI runs on demo-ai and talks to the vLLM router. The model it offers is Qwen 2.5 0.5B Instruct.

00:00:23.510 --> 00:00:28.630
Ask the model a question and press Enter.

00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:36.100
vLLM generates the answer on the NVIDIA T400 that SUSE Virtualization passes through to the GPU worker.

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:39.960
Send a second, longer prompt to give the GPU more work.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:48.340
The second answer streams in the same way.

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:58.640
An OpenTelemetry Collector on demo-ai sends vLLM and GPU metrics to SUSE Observability. Under SUSE AI, the vLLM engine of demo-ai is listed.

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:04.990
In the metrics explorer, query the GPU memory in use that the NVIDIA DCGM exporter reports for demo-ai.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:12.000
Narrow the time range to the last fifteen minutes.

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:20.210
vLLM keeps a little over three of the T400's four gigabytes in use, for the model weights and its KV cache.

00:01:20.210 --> 00:01:24.360
Now query vLLM's own metric, the generated tokens per second.

00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:35.400
The token rate rises while the model generates the answers you just sent, and falls back to zero when it is idle.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:44.790
The model answers from the passed-through GPU, and SUSE Observability shows the GPU and vLLM metrics. Next, you add a GPU worker.
